# Managed app data

Prefer `@unstoppable-stack/p2p/data` for new JSON datasets when the installed
package exports it (alpha.2 or later). Use installed types and the app's recorded
compatibility profile. Never claim an unpublished candidate is on npm. Generated
Node/desktop apps include SHARED-DATA.md, a shared-data module and
examples/share-data.mjs. Expo remains deferred; do not import this native module
in browser/React renderers or assume mobile bundling is validated.

```js
import { openCollection } from '@unstoppable-stack/p2p/data'
const notes = await openCollection({ storage: './shared-notes' })
await notes.put('hello', { text: 'Hello!' })
await notes.network.ready() // only before advertising; local open is offline-safe
console.log({ key: notes.key, version: notes.version })
// Keep open for peers; at application shutdown: await notes.close()
```

Another device opens its own directory with `key`, calls `sync({ version,
signal, timeoutMs })`, then `get('hello')`. One writer owns mutations. Shared
keys permit reading, not writing; RPC grant revocation does not revoke data keys.
A local-downloaded checkpoint is not a backup claim or acknowledgement of future
writes. Discovery retries independently and reports network timeout diagnostics.

For several writers, explicitly install `autobase@7.28.2` and use
`openCollaborativeDatabase({ storage, key? })`. The founder alone calls
`members.add(writerKey)` after verifying that key. Joiners wait for `writable`
before put/del. get processes available incoming history. Same-key replacements
use the last operation in Autobase order, not wall-clock order or text merging.
The founder remains the sole indexer; collaborative storage is experimental.

Use a dedicated directory per dataset/device. Saved keys reopen automatically.
Never silently point a managed API at an existing Hyperbee, Autobee or HyperDB
store or regenerate edited apps. For existing schema-based storage, keep its
engine, reducer and replication contracts; plan an explicit migration separately.
For custom reducers use `/db`; for hosting existing cores/drives use `/sync`.
Do not add a second swarm or Corestore around a managed dataset.

Test persistence offline, writer admission/removal, cancellation and sustained
connections. Public-device validation requires real network evidence: local
loopback tests and Node subprocesses do not prove NAT traversal, packaged Bare
or mobile acceptance. No reachable DHT peers can indicate blocked UDP; do not
silently disable security controls or promise an HTTP fallback.

Guides: https://unstoppable-stack.dev/docs/p2p/sync and
https://unstoppable-stack.dev/docs/p2p/database
